5. Module requirements

Overview

Make Run audit, Import data, Plan shifts, and Create task easy to find. Show up to four useful metrics calculated from actual selected data, with period context and links to records. Show a compact Needs attention list only for real, actionable findings; omit it when there is nothing to show. Remove duplicate module marketing cards, fabricated alerts, countdowns, and the prominent backup control.

Audits

Default to channel, interaction reference, analyst, date, transcript, and Run audit. After execution show score, verdict, actual findings, and supporting evidence. Keep history, scoring rubric, coaching, exports, and create-task actions available through concise secondary views.

Make the rule-based engine transparent. Never describe keyword rules as an LLM assessment. Never award a general criterion a pass merely because the transcript is long. Unsupported criteria must be Not assessed or Needs review. Evidence quotations must be exact substrings of the source with offsets/line references. Never fabricate quotations or infer consent from a product name alone. Show automated security flags for human review and retain configurable fatal rules with clear justification. Preserve reviewer overrides with reason, original result, and timestamp.

Hide unsupported audio/transcription controls. If an optional transcription provider is implemented, execute a real request and handle failure honestly; otherwise the user pastes a transcript. Do not display Ready for transcription as if transcription has occurred.

Operations, Insights, and Reconciliation

Provide Overview, Insights, and Reconciliation tabs sharing team/date filters and the same normalized dataset. Calculate volume, resolved/open, resolution rate, SLA compliance, SDR, and duration metrics from declared definitions. Show denominators and exclusions in a compact metric-details view.

Implement operational-day calculation using configured timezone and cutoff, including Asia/Kolkata at 07:00. Parse date-only values and local timestamps explicitly; do not accidentally interpret them as UTC. Test cutoff boundaries and overnight cases. Use the current clock or selected reporting date, not a fixed date.

Preserve validated anomaly detection, category contribution, trends, and period comparisons. Make comparison dates selectable, use complete/matched observation windows, and disclose insufficient baselines or incomplete periods. Generate narratives from calculated results only. Do not claim a root cause from correlation without supporting records. The global assistant replaces the existing canned Q&A panel.

Reconciliation imports or accepts actual expected counts. Display match/mismatch/unresolved with source rows and rule definitions. Preview any push into Performance and show exactly which employee, period, and metric will change. Invalid/unmapped rows must never silently affect official results.

Shifts

Default to team, week, editable roster, Validate, and Export. Put Coverage, Fairness, and What-if planning in secondary tabs/panels. Keep leave, comp-off, shift definitions, and workforce policy controls.

Calculate staffing from imported interval demand, actual AHT/concurrency, configured service targets, shrinkage, and occupancy. Remove constructed hourly demand patterns from production calculations. Missing demand means coverage is unavailable, not automatically healthy. Distinguish headcount, available capacity, and modeled staffing demand.

Preserve and verify Erlang C for applicable voice queues and appropriate chat/throughput models. Validate unsupported or infeasible inputs. Treat results as planning estimates, with assumptions visible. Policy settings must actually control checks. Validate overnight rest, leave conflicts, maximum days per week, and consecutive-day runs across week boundaries. Invalid edits must be blocked or saved only as a visibly invalid draft, never approved implicitly.

Roster generation/optimization may propose a validated draft; never claim an optimal schedule without an optimizer. Keep baseline and proposed schedules separate. Persist edits, leave, scenarios, and validation context. Detect stale draft revisions before applying changes.

Tasks

Default to a compact table: title, owner, status, priority, due date. Provide Board as an alternate view and Timeline as optional. Keep search, filtering, sorting, subtasks, and cross-module source links. Support create, update, assign, complete, reopen, and archive. Use collision-resistant IDs and dynamic dates. Support keyboard alternatives to drag and drop. Prevent duplicate tasks from the same finding unless explicitly requested.

Performance

Default to selected team/month, employee results, and drill-down score breakdown. Keep Inputs and Scoring rules in tabs. Preserve the existing 25-parameter rubric where valid, with editable weights/bands, directionality, tie handling, version, and explanations. No hardcoded analyst count or September benchmark.

Missing performance values must remain missing, not score as zero automatically. Make completeness and scoring eligibility explicit. Version rubric changes and record recalculation history. Spreadsheet benchmark validation must compare actual parsed values with clear tolerances and mismatches; otherwise show Not tested. Do not fabricate a pass.

Team Directory

Default to a unified searchable table: employee ID, name, team, role, manager, location, and active status. Preserve all original sheets and source columns in a source-data view using a sheet selector instead of a long tab row. Deduplicate using normalized stable employee IDs while preserving memberships. Flag conflicting identities rather than silently merging. Show employee details and linked tasks/audits/shifts/performance in a side panel. Prevent orphaned assignments when archiving employees.

Settings

Sections: Data Management, Operational Policies, Appearance, Assistant, Backup/Restore, and collapsed Advanced Diagnostics. Remove Run Test Gates from the global header. Keep technical status out of normal working flows.

